WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.890
Breaking Bad is one of the best shows in TV
history, and after five seasons, it all led

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.880
to an ending that people still talk about
today.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:11.220
But if you need a helping hand to understand
that final episode, well then, let's cook.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.959
Creator Vince Gilligan had one of the best
high-concept hooks for Breaking Bad that any

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:16.880
show has ever had.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.000
As Gilligan put it:

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:22.660
"This is a story about a man who transforms
himself from Mr. Chips into Scarface."

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.210
The appeal of Breaking Bad is pretty simple
because it asks a question we've all probably

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:27.340
wondered at least once.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:32.150
How far would a regular person, a Mr. Chips
type, go if they knew they were dying and

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:33.140
had to provide for their family?

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:37.460
With that theme in mind, it's easy to see
how Breaking Bad is about the American dream,

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:41.550
the idea that if you do good work, then the
people in charge will see your value and reward

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:42.400
you for your skills.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:46.020
Of course, most people would prefer their
paychecks come from legitimate bosses and

00:00:46.039 --> 00:00:48.929
not various criminal drug lords in the crystal
meth trade.

00:00:48.929 --> 00:00:50.120
Not so for Walter White.

00:00:50.329 --> 00:00:53.239
After all, the longer he stays in the game,
the higher he rises.

00:00:53.239 --> 00:00:56.809
But the higher he rises, it gets harder and
harder for him to argue that he's just leaving

00:00:56.809 --> 00:01:01.239
a nest egg for his family, and he transforms
from a sympathetic and unlikely anti-hero

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:02.800
into a full-fledged villain.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.200
"Say my name."

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:12.560
"Heisenberg."

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:17.840
So we've spent a lot of time talking about
Walt, but what does the actual ending of Breaking

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:18.420
Bad mean?

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.560
Well, first we need to specify which ending
we're talking about.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:26.469
Each of the final three episodes, "Ozymandias,"
"Granite State," and "Felina", can function

00:01:26.469 --> 00:01:27.880
as a kind of ending for the show.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:31.320
In the case of "Ozymandias," it's a poetic,
circular ending.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.720
"My name is Ozymandias, king of kings!

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.700
Look on my works, ye mighty, and despair."

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:45.020
Walt betrays Jesse completely by giving his
former partner up to the white supremacists

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:45.920
he's been working with.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:50.060
Worse still, he admits to Jesse that he watched
Jane die and did nothing.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.560
"I watched her overdose and choke to death.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:57.740
I could have saved her.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:01:59.780
But I didn't."

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:04.900
But even before handing Jesse over to the
Nazis, Walt is indirectly responsible for

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.700
getting Hank, his DEA brother-in-law, killed.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.140
And then Walt has to watch as Uncle Jack and
his white supremacist gang take away all his

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.400
earnings, except one single barrel of cash.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:18.480
Walt then has to roll the barrel back towards
civilization, passing by the pair of pants

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:19.980
he lost in the very first episode.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.060
When he tries to convince his family to flee
with him, his wife and son attack him, and

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:25.700
Walt kidnaps his infant daughter.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.180
It's all of Walt's lies laid bare.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.370
He chose an empire over his family, and like
the Shelley poem the episode is named after

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:34.300
says, no empire lasts forever.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.740
Walt has the money to flee into a kind of
witness protection program for criminals,

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:40.760
but he's giving up the family that he pretended
to do so much for.

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:45.799
If "Ozymandias" is the "realistic" end to
Walt's journey, then "Granite State" is a

00:02:45.799 --> 00:02:47.950
fitting punishment for the former meth kingpin.

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:52.819
Walt ends up in a remote cabin where his only
company, Ed "the Disappearer," comes by just

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:54.680
once a month to drop off supplies.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:58.640
Even in the kind of purgatory that Walt lives
in, he's still not free of his cancer, and

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.660
he's constantly waiting for Ed to return with
chemotherapy drugs.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:05.620
It's a fitting punishment for a man who pretended
to do everything he did out of loyalty and

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:06.880
family obligations.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:11.520
Walt is so lonely that he ends up paying Ed
thousands of dollars just for an extra hour

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:12.220
of his time.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:14.780
He's alive and rich but all alone.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:19.260
Much like the characters of a different AMC
show, Walt is basically the walking dead.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.480
The only thing that brings him out of his
mountain retreat is the possibility of passing

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:26.159
on his ill-gotten gains to his family.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.980
Walt literally tries to buy his way back into
his son's affections, and of course, it doesn't

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:30.480
work.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:35.099
However, he's given one last goal when he
sees his former colleagues Gretchen and Elliott

00:03:35.099 --> 00:03:40.050
on television, downplaying Walt's contributions
to their company, Gray Matter Technologies.

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:41.580
It's reasonable that they would.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:45.600
After all, it's not exactly good for a company's
stock to have a co-founder become a murderous

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:46.380
drug kingpin.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.800
Walt, unsurprisingly, does not see their side
of things.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.960
Instead, he thinks they're disrespecting him
by not giving him credit.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:58.140
"Felina," the finale episode of Breaking Bad,
is basically a victory lap for Walt.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:01.680
He gets to accomplish everything that he left
his mountain retreat to do.

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:05.739
He threatens Gretchen and Elliott into giving
all of his money to his family under the guise

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:07.060
of a charitable donation.

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:09.540
"Gretchen, would you mind?

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:11.780
I don't wanna lose any of it under the furniture.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:16.640
Alright."

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:21.000
He kills all of the white supremacists that
stole his blue meth recipe.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.940
Walt frees Jesse from slavery, and he even
gets a kind of closure with Skyler and his

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:25.540
infant daughter.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:30.580
Plus, he poisons Lydia with a whole lot of
ricin, and he successfully evades the police

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:31.660
all the way to the end.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:34.700
Even when he appears to die, it's on his own
terms.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:38.750
He's not rotting of cancer but dying in the
arms of his true love, the chemistry lab that

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:40.000
produces his blue meth.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:44.639
Walt is infamous, which as we know from The
Three Amigos, is when you're more than famous.

00:04:44.639 --> 00:04:48.750
Combine all of these facts, and it's hard
to deny that this is Walter's perfect, inevitable

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:49.400
ending.

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:54.060
"Felina" might be Walt's fantasy, but it's
everyone else's nightmare.

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:56.690
Throughout the episode, Walt is a monstrous
presence.

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:59.130
He moves through scenes like a horror movie
slasher.

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:03.020
When he talks to Skyler, he appears like a
ghost, the scene is even blocked like a jump

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:03.520
scare.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:06.740
When he brags to Lydia about how he poisoned
her drink, it's a moment that wouldn't be

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:08.660
out of place in a Final Destination movie.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.920
Walt gets his victory lap in the final episode,
but it's only because he's chasing his enemies

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:13.680
around the track.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:18.000
"I slipped it into that Stevia crap that you're
always putting in your tea."

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:19.780
"Oh my god."

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:26.020
Even when Jesse escapes the white supremacist
compound, finally free of Walt and his vindictive

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:30.410
torturers, he lets out a guttural scream straight
from The Texas Chainsaw Massacre.

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:34.240
If there's anyone in the show that knows what
kind of monster Walt was, it's Jesse.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:38.800
When he drives out of the show, it's as viscerally
satisfying as seeing the final girl in a horror

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:39.860
movie survive the killer.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:54.700
Walt starts Breaking Bad by telling his family
that he loves them, and that everything he

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:56.180
did was for their sake.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:00.810
He ends the show hobbling out to the meth
shed to be with what he truly loves: his work,

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:01.460
his legacy.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:03.820
It's clear that he was never really Mr. Chips.

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:07.380
Whatever falling out happened between him
and his partners before the show started was

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:09.040
a symptom of something deeper.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:13.320
Walt's cancer was just an excuse for him to
be the power-hungry villain he'd always wanted

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:13.820
to be.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:15.260
As Vince Gilligan once put it:

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:19.570
"We always say in the writers' room, if Walter
White has a true superpower, it's not his

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:23.620
knowledge of chemistry or his intellect, it's
his ability to lie to himself.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:25.280
He is the world's greatest liar."

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:29.680
Walt lied to himself for so long that he believed
his own lies for most of the show.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:31.040
He lied to the audience, too.

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:35.300
How much you want to believe that Walt was
a good man is dependent on how much you want

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:36.480
to believe his lies.

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:40.590
Was he a good man who "broke bad," or was
he always a twisted monster who finally got

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:42.720
the excuse to behave like Scarface?

